WEBVTT

00:00:02.786 --> 00:00:04.160
<v Nora>Good morning, Theo!

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.922
<v Theo>Happy Friday, November twentieth, twenty twenty-six.

00:00:07.922 --> 00:00:12.190
<v Nora>Five stories today: three in neurotech, two in health tech.

00:00:12.190 --> 00:00:16.675
<v Theo>First up, BCI and Neuromodulation Strategy. Story one of five.

00:00:16.675 --> 00:00:25.718
<v Nora>Synchron is voluntarily pausing enrollment in its INTENT clinical trial to upgrade a hardware component on its brain implant.

00:00:25.718 --> 00:00:30.782
<v Theo>Right, so... this is for their second-generation Stentrode BCI system.

00:00:30.782 --> 00:00:40.765
<v Theo>They identified a performance issue with the lead—that's the physical cable carrying signals from the implanted device to the electronics.

00:00:40.765 --> 00:00:44.816
<v Nora>Wait, so is the issue specifically with that lead cable?

00:00:44.816 --> 00:00:51.617
<v Theo>Exactly, it's a performance issue with the lead carrying signals to the implanted electronics.

00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:58.706
<v Theo>If the wiring degrades or introduces noise, your signal quality drops and signal processing fails.

00:00:58.706 --> 00:01:01.527
<v Nora>Honestly, I think this is a smart move.

00:01:01.527 --> 00:01:11.077
<v Nora>Pausing a clinical trial to fix hardware takes guts, but it's way better than enrolling dozens more patients and getting noisy data.

00:01:11.077 --> 00:01:12.596
<v Theo>I mean... fair point.

00:01:12.596 --> 00:01:20.264
<v Theo>In chronic implantable devices, mechanical leads and interconnects are notorious single points of failure.

00:01:20.264 --> 00:01:28.207
<v Theo>If you don't solve lead fatigue now, you are looking at signal loss or explants two years down the road.

00:01:28.207 --> 00:01:34.012
<v Nora>Exactly, taking a pause now sets them up for long-term clinical reliability.

00:01:34.012 --> 00:01:35.387
<v Theo>Story two of five.

00:01:35.387 --> 00:01:46.003
<v Theo>Cognito Therapeutics is pushing for FDA clearance for its Alzheimer's device using trial subgroup data after missing its primary endpoints.

00:01:46.003 --> 00:01:49.822
<v Nora>Okay, this one is a high-stakes regulatory gamble.

00:01:49.822 --> 00:01:58.758
<v Nora>Cognito ran their Phase Three HOPE trial for Spectris AD—their non-invasive device for early symptomatic Alzheimer's.

00:01:58.758 --> 00:02:07.541
<v Theo>Right, but across the total trial population, it failed to show benefit in slowing cognitive or functional decline.

00:02:07.541 --> 00:02:10.520
<v Nora>But... here is where they are pivoting.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:20.602
<v Nora>In a prespecified subgroup of patients with mild Alzheimer's, they saw slower declines in cognitive abilities and daily functioning.

00:02:20.602 --> 00:02:29.691
<v Theo>Look... I get why Cognito is trying this, especially with their Breakthrough Device designation from twenty twenty-one.

00:02:29.691 --> 00:02:37.558
<v Theo>But relying on subgroup analysis after missing your primary endpoint is a classic Hail Mary at the FDA.

00:02:37.558 --> 00:02:46.112
<v Theo>Once you start slicing trial demographics after the main readout misses, statistical rigor gets questioned fast.

00:02:46.112 --> 00:02:56.041
<v Nora>True, but if the mild subgroup was prespecified in their protocol, regulators might consider a narrow, specific target population.

00:02:56.041 --> 00:03:03.220
<v Theo>Maybe... but it proves how unforgiving late-stage clinical endpoints are in neurodegeneration.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:07.315
<v Nora>Moving to New Products and Probes. Story three of five.

00:03:07.315 --> 00:03:16.101
<v Nora>Axoft is showing real clinical momentum with its ultra-soft Fleuron BCI probes designed to blend directly into tissue.

00:03:16.101 --> 00:03:20.122
<v Theo>Okay... I'll admit it. That's clever material science.

00:03:20.122 --> 00:03:26.674
<v Theo>The fundamental problem with chronic brain implants has always been mechanical mismatch.

00:03:26.674 --> 00:03:32.630
<v Theo>Rigid probes can be orders of magnitude stiffer than soft cortical brain tissue.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:43.128
<v Nora>Right, so whenever the brain moves inside the skull, rigid microelectrodes can irritate surrounding tissue, causing scarring and signal loss!

00:03:43.128 --> 00:03:50.499
<v Theo>Exactly. Axoft's Fleuron probes are ultra-soft, engineered to better match native tissue mechanics.

00:03:50.499 --> 00:04:01.742
<v Theo>By minimizing physical strain at the neural interface, soft probes aim to preserve long-term signal quality without triggering strong immune responses.

00:04:01.742 --> 00:04:02.784
<v Nora>Which is huge!

00:04:02.784 --> 00:04:10.974
<v Nora>Solving chronic tissue integration is the holy grail for keeping BCIs functional for decades instead of years.

00:04:10.974 --> 00:04:14.846
<v Theo>Now, do it outside a lab in long-term human studies.

00:04:14.846 --> 00:04:23.855
<v Theo>I want to see long-term signal stability and electrode reliability data, but the engineering foundation is really strong.

00:04:23.855 --> 00:04:29.513
<v Nora>Tissue-matched probes could finally unlock truly permanent brain interfaces.

00:04:29.513 --> 00:04:35.916
<v Nora>That's the neuro probes. Now, Digital Health and Commercial Scale. Story four of five.

00:04:35.916 --> 00:04:44.587
<v Theo>Telehealth giants Hims and Hers and Noom are officially entering the consumer peptide market with new prescription offerings.

00:04:44.587 --> 00:04:54.715
<v Nora>Yeah, both platforms announced programs offering synthetic peptides like BPC-one-fifty-seven for health optimization, energy, and muscle recovery.

00:04:54.715 --> 00:05:05.189
<v Theo>This shift happened because the Department of Health and Human Services relaxed compounding pharmacy restrictions around these short-chain amino acids.

00:05:05.189 --> 00:05:10.253
<v Nora>And it opens up a massive high-margin wellness stream for both companies!

00:05:10.253 --> 00:05:18.924
<v Nora>Hims has a market cap of more than three point five billion dollars, and Noom is valued at three point seven billion dollars.

00:05:18.924 --> 00:05:26.485
<v Nora>They already have huge customer bases, so adding popular peptides is an instant booster for revenue per user.

00:05:26.485 --> 00:05:28.081
<v Theo>Wait, hold on a second.

00:05:28.081 --> 00:05:38.764
<v Theo>Synthetic peptides are short-chain amino acids, but many of these compounded formulations lack randomized controlled trials for human safety and efficacy.

00:05:38.764 --> 00:05:48.475
<v Theo>Handing out unapproved peptides through mass-market telehealth channels carries real clinical and regulatory risk if adverse events surface.

00:05:48.475 --> 00:05:58.048
<v Nora>Fair point on clinical evidence, but commercially, it shows how quickly digital health giants will pivot when regulatory doors swing open.

00:05:58.048 --> 00:05:59.366
<v Theo>Story five of five.

00:05:59.366 --> 00:06:09.008
<v Nora>DarioHealth is partnering with WebMD Health Services to plug its multi-condition management platform into sixty-five million covered lives.

00:06:09.008 --> 00:06:20.870
<v Theo>Dario is integrating its DarioIQ AI engine and clinical care modules directly into WebMD ONE, which reaches over thirty-five hundred enterprise employers and health plans.

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:32.177
<v Nora>They're covering cardiometabolic health, behavioral support, musculoskeletal care, sleep apnea, and GLP-one programs, with revenue starting in twenty twenty-seven.

00:06:32.177 --> 00:06:35.948
<v Theo>What I like here is the enterprise distribution model.

00:06:35.948 --> 00:06:45.515
<v Theo>Instead of burning millions on direct-to-consumer ad spending, Dario is piggybacking directly on WebMD's existing channel infrastructure.

00:06:45.515 --> 00:06:52.499
<v Nora>Exactly! Direct-to-consumer health tech customer acquisition costs are brutally expensive right now.

00:06:52.499 --> 00:07:04.789
<v Nora>Partnering with an enterprise anchor gives Dario massive distribution, while WebMD gets a platform that claims to cut total payer healthcare costs by up to ten percent in-year.

00:07:04.789 --> 00:07:14.287
<v Theo>The open question is long-term member engagement across multiple conditions, but as a commercial distribution strategy, it's very clean.

00:07:14.287 --> 00:07:21.480
<v Nora>Piggybacking on established enterprise channels is becoming the gold standard for digital health scale.

00:07:21.480 --> 00:07:23.714
<v Theo>That brings us to market trends.

00:07:23.714 --> 00:07:36.913
<v Nora>The big theme today? Companies across neurotech and digital health are stepping away from rigid, all-or-nothing bets to favor flexible commercial distribution and iterative hardware pivots.

00:07:36.913 --> 00:07:46.829
<v Theo>Exactly. Synchron paused its trial to upgrade physical leads, and Cognito pivoted toward mild Alzheimer's subgroup data after endpoint misses.

00:07:46.829 --> 00:07:57.863
<v Nora>Meanwhile, Hims and Noom jumped on immediate regulatory shifts in compounding, and Dario partnered with WebMD to reach enterprise lives without high ad spend.

00:07:57.863 --> 00:08:03.170
<v Theo>Capital efficiency and strategic flexibility are winning over raw cash burn.

00:08:03.170 --> 00:08:08.478
<v Nora>Here is today's career signal for biomedical engineers and product managers.

00:08:08.478 --> 00:08:18.464
<v Theo>When developing medical devices or digital health platforms, design mid-course iteration milestones directly into your initial project roadmap.

00:08:18.464 --> 00:08:25.307
<v Nora>Relying on post-hoc subgroup analysis to rescue a failed clinical trial is a huge regulatory risk.

00:08:25.307 --> 00:08:36.263
<v Theo>Building hardware iteration checkpoints and commercial flexibility early keeps your platform resilient when real-world clinical friction hits.

00:08:36.263 --> 00:08:39.349
<v Nora>Let me do a quick recap of today's show.

00:08:39.349 --> 00:08:46.138
<v Theo>One: Synchron pauses its BCI trial to upgrade lead hardware before expanding enrollment.

00:08:46.138 --> 00:08:55.474
<v Nora>Two: Cognito pushes for FDA clearance of its Alzheimer's device using mild subgroup data after missing primary endpoints.

00:08:55.474 --> 00:09:04.269
<v Theo>Three: Axoft advances its ultra-soft Fleuron probes to solve chronic tissue mechanical mismatch in brain implants.

00:09:04.269 --> 00:09:11.598
<v Nora>Four: Hims and Noom expand into clinical peptides following relaxed compounding pharmacy rules.

00:09:11.598 --> 00:09:21.551
<v Theo>Five: DarioHealth partners with WebMD Health Services to distribute its multi-condition platform across enterprise covered lives.

00:09:21.551 --> 00:09:29.883
<v Nora>The bottom line today? Flexibility in engineering and distribution is what keeps health tech moving forward.

00:09:29.883 --> 00:09:31.426
<v Theo>Catch you on Monday.

00:09:31.426 --> 00:09:33.047
<v Nora>Have a great weekend!
